1
00:00:01,027 --> 00:00:04,027
You already know what the
Riemann hypothesis says.

2
00:00:04,147 --> 00:00:06,667
So our question is more specific:

3
00:00:06,707 --> 00:00:10,707
what would it mean to exclude every
zero to the right of one fixed

4
00:00:10,707 --> 00:00:14,707
vertical line? We will explain the
claims of these two manuscripts,

5
00:00:14,707 --> 00:00:18,587
and then actually derive where
eleven twelfths comes from.

6
00:00:18,587 --> 00:00:22,467
This is an explanation of their
argument, not an independent proof

7
00:00:22,467 --> 00:00:22,947
audit.

8
00:00:24,993 --> 00:00:28,673
Write a complex number as sigma plus i t.

9
00:00:28,673 --> 00:00:32,073
Sigma is horizontal; t is vertical.

10
00:00:32,073 --> 00:00:36,113
This surface shows actual numerical
values of the zeta function, with

11
00:00:36,113 --> 00:00:39,473
height equal to log of
one plus its magnitude.

12
00:00:39,473 --> 00:00:43,833
Valleys mark small values. The
highlighted point is the first known

13
00:00:43,873 --> 00:00:48,473
nontrivial zero. A finite plot
illustrates the function, but cannot

14
00:00:48,593 --> 00:00:50,593
establish a statement at every height.

15
00:00:52,547 --> 00:00:57,027
Classical zero-free regions narrow
toward the line sigma equals one

16
00:00:57,027 --> 00:01:01,387
as the height increases. These
papers claim fixed boundaries:

17
00:01:01,387 --> 00:01:05,267
eleven twelfths and, more
strongly, seven eighths.

18
00:01:05,267 --> 00:01:08,107
The second excludes a wider half-plane.

19
00:01:08,227 --> 00:01:12,227
Reflection symmetry would then confine
the nontrivial zeta zeros between

20
00:01:12,227 --> 00:01:14,227
one eighth and seven eighths.

21
00:01:14,227 --> 00:01:17,027
The critical line remains
a much stronger target.

22
00:01:19,559 --> 00:01:24,999
For sigma greater than one, zeta is
both a sum over all positive integers

23
00:01:24,999 --> 00:01:27,359
and a product over primes.

24
00:01:27,359 --> 00:01:31,359
Expanding each geometric series in
the product lists every integer

25
00:01:31,399 --> 00:01:34,839
exactly once, by unique prime factorization.

26
00:01:34,839 --> 00:01:37,399
This is the entry point for primes.

27
00:01:37,399 --> 00:01:40,999
Inside the critical strip we
use analytic continuation;

28
00:01:40,999 --> 00:01:44,919
the displayed infinite product is not
being asserted to converge there.

29
00:01:46,879 --> 00:01:50,799
A Dirichlet character is a
periodic multiplicative weight.

30
00:01:50,879 --> 00:01:56,279
For example, this character modulo
four assigns zero to even integers,

31
00:01:56,279 --> 00:02:01,039
plus one to integers congruent to one,
and minus one to integers congruent

32
00:02:01,039 --> 00:02:05,159
to three. Inserting these
weights gives an L function.

33
00:02:05,239 --> 00:02:09,119
Comparing such weighted prime sums
helps separate primes in different

34
00:02:09,119 --> 00:02:10,559
arithmetic progressions.

35
00:02:12,569 --> 00:02:16,272
The manuscripts claim their boundary
for every Dirichlet character,

36
00:02:16,272 --> 00:02:19,152
not just the trivial
character that gives zeta.

37
00:02:19,152 --> 00:02:22,912
The same numerical boundary works
independently of the conductor and

38
00:02:22,912 --> 00:02:27,392
the height. That does not mean every
constant in the proof is uniform:

39
00:02:27,392 --> 00:02:30,472
constants may depend on the
fixed target character.

40
00:02:30,472 --> 00:02:35,072
It is the exponent, and ultimately the
boundary, that must remain common.

41
00:02:37,544 --> 00:02:42,504
The Möbius function is zero when a
square of a prime divides the integer.

42
00:02:42,504 --> 00:02:47,104
Otherwise it is minus one to the
number of distinct prime factors.

43
00:02:47,224 --> 00:02:52,184
Thus six has value plus one, thirty
has value minus one, and twelve

44
00:02:52,184 --> 00:02:57,144
has value zero. For sigma greater
than one, replacing each Euler factor

45
00:02:57,144 --> 00:03:00,584
by its inverse produces the
series for one over zeta.

46
00:03:02,704 --> 00:03:06,544
Let M of x be the cumulative
sum of these signs.

47
00:03:06,544 --> 00:03:11,024
This curve is computed from actual
Möbius values, not a simulated random

48
00:03:11,024 --> 00:03:14,264
walk. The trivial bound is order x.

49
00:03:14,264 --> 00:03:18,984
A bound of x to the power alpha, with
alpha less than one, would quantify

50
00:03:18,984 --> 00:03:23,504
cancellation. We cannot infer that
bound from a short plotted sample.

51
00:03:25,477 --> 00:03:30,597
Partial summation writes the reciprocal
of zeta as s times an integral

52
00:03:30,597 --> 00:03:35,277
of M of x against x to the
power minus s minus one.

53
00:03:35,277 --> 00:03:39,957
If M grows no faster than x to alpha,
the integral converges locally

54
00:03:39,957 --> 00:03:42,757
uniformly when sigma exceeds alpha.

55
00:03:42,757 --> 00:03:45,797
It therefore defines a
holomorphic function there.

56
00:03:45,797 --> 00:03:49,677
But a zero of zeta would force
its reciprocal to have a pole.

57
00:03:49,677 --> 00:03:51,877
Those two facts cannot coexist.

58
00:03:54,396 --> 00:03:58,703
The argument works in the number
field Q of square root minus three.

59
00:03:58,983 --> 00:04:04,223
Its integers are a plus b omega,
where omega is a cube root of one.

60
00:04:04,583 --> 00:04:08,103
In the complex plane they
form this triangular lattice.

61
00:04:08,183 --> 00:04:12,463
The norm is a squared
minus a b plus b squared.

62
00:04:12,463 --> 00:04:16,703
The geometry makes cubic and sextic
residue characters natural tools

63
00:04:16,703 --> 00:04:17,623
for the problem.

64
00:04:19,576 --> 00:04:24,136
The paper sums over nonzero integral
ideals, with an ideal version

65
00:04:24,136 --> 00:04:27,776
of Möbius and a finite-order
Hecke character, nu.

66
00:04:28,216 --> 00:04:31,816
You can think of nu as the multiplicative
phase weight in this larger

67
00:04:31,816 --> 00:04:37,136
arithmetic setting. A smooth window
W selects norms near a scale D.

68
00:04:37,496 --> 00:04:40,976
The resulting sum A one has roughly D terms.

69
00:04:41,336 --> 00:04:45,536
Primes above two and three and the
conductor are excluded as specified

70
00:04:45,536 --> 00:04:46,296
in the paper.

71
00:04:48,323 --> 00:04:53,323
Now insert a sextic residue character
depending on a new variable u.

72
00:04:53,323 --> 00:04:57,843
Its nonzero values are sixth roots
of one, shown around this circle.

73
00:04:57,843 --> 00:05:01,443
Changing u produces a family of sums A u.

74
00:05:01,443 --> 00:05:04,803
The original target is the row u equals one.

75
00:05:04,803 --> 00:05:08,523
The strategy is to control the
combined squared size of the entire

76
00:05:08,763 --> 00:05:11,363
family, then recover that particular row.

77
00:05:13,921 --> 00:05:17,521
Here is the difficult estimate
the October paper claims.

78
00:05:17,761 --> 00:05:22,321
Sum the squared magnitudes
of A u over norms up to H.

79
00:05:22,321 --> 00:05:27,001
The answer is at most a constant
times D to one plus epsilon, times

80
00:05:27,041 --> 00:05:32,001
H, with H equal to D to one
plus a small positive theta.

81
00:05:32,001 --> 00:05:36,201
Since there are order H rows, the
average squared magnitude is about

82
00:05:36,201 --> 00:05:38,881
D, allowing a tiny exponent loss.

83
00:05:40,841 --> 00:05:46,001
The bars are a schematic energy
budget, not computed character sums.

84
00:05:46,081 --> 00:05:49,681
Average square-root cancellation
does not say that every individual

85
00:05:49,721 --> 00:05:52,041
sum has size square root D.

86
00:05:52,041 --> 00:05:55,561
A few large outliers could
fit inside the total budget.

87
00:05:55,561 --> 00:06:00,481
This distinction matters: extracting
the target row using only a typical-row

88
00:06:00,641 --> 00:06:02,121
argument would be invalid.

89
00:06:04,075 --> 00:06:08,675
The next move is to find many
rows that almost reproduce A one.

90
00:06:08,675 --> 00:06:12,835
If A one were huge, all those
rows would consume energy.

91
00:06:12,835 --> 00:06:16,342
The total mean-square estimate
then limits its size.

92
00:06:16,342 --> 00:06:19,915
The sixth-power rows provide
exactly this repetition.

93
00:06:19,915 --> 00:06:23,995
That is the bridge from an average
theorem to a bound on one prescribed

94
00:06:23,995 --> 00:06:24,875
L function.

95
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:32,500
Take u equal to p to the sixth
power, where p is a prime element in

96
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:37,180
the Eisenstein integers. Away from
p, a sixth-order character raised

97
00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:39,420
to the sixth power is one.

98
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:42,900
When p divides the summation
index, it is zero.

99
00:06:43,054 --> 00:06:47,540
So A of p to the sixth differs from
A one only through those omitted

100
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:52,260
multiples. For prime norm about Y,
their total contribution is bounded

101
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:54,060
by order D over Y.

102
00:06:56,064 --> 00:07:00,567
A prime with norm at most Y gives a
sixth-power row with norm at most

103
00:07:00,567 --> 00:07:05,047
Y to the sixth. Set Y equal
to H to the one sixth.

104
00:07:05,047 --> 00:07:09,687
The prime ideal theorem supplies
order Y over log Y such rows.

105
00:07:09,967 --> 00:07:12,847
Divide the full energy budget by this number.

106
00:07:12,847 --> 00:07:16,647
Squaring the omission error gives
the second term displayed here.

107
00:07:16,647 --> 00:07:19,527
Logarithms are absorbed into D to epsilon.

108
00:07:21,473 --> 00:07:26,793
Ignoring only the arbitrarily small
exponent losses, H is D to one

109
00:07:26,833 --> 00:07:31,473
plus theta. The first term
is D times H to five sixths.

110
00:07:31,473 --> 00:07:35,553
Its exponent is eleven sixths
plus five theta over six.

111
00:07:35,553 --> 00:07:37,393
Now take the square root...

112
00:07:37,393 --> 00:07:40,433
eleven twelfths plus five theta over twelve.

113
00:07:40,433 --> 00:07:44,753
The error contributes exponent five
sixths minus theta over six, which

114
00:07:44,753 --> 00:07:45,553
is smaller.

115
00:07:47,507 --> 00:07:52,227
For any requested positive epsilon,
choose theta sufficiently small,

116
00:07:52,227 --> 00:07:56,307
and then choose the loss in the
mean-square estimate sufficiently small.

117
00:07:56,307 --> 00:07:59,787
This yields D to eleven
twelfths plus epsilon.

118
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:02,627
We have not set theta equal to zero;

119
00:08:02,627 --> 00:08:04,867
later recursion needs it positive.

120
00:08:04,867 --> 00:08:07,827
The constant may worsen as epsilon shrinks.

121
00:08:07,827 --> 00:08:11,747
The zero-free boundary is the open
half-plane strictly beyond eleven

122
00:08:11,747 --> 00:08:12,467
twelfths.

123
00:08:14,995 --> 00:08:19,898
The paper uses smooth sums, so the
natural transform is Mellin rather

124
00:08:19,898 --> 00:08:22,578
than the sharp-cutoff
integral we saw earlier.

125
00:08:22,778 --> 00:08:28,498
Integrate A one of D against D to
minus s, with measure d D over D.

126
00:08:28,938 --> 00:08:33,258
The cancellation bound gives convergence
at large D when sigma exceeds

127
00:08:33,258 --> 00:08:37,458
eleven twelfths. Compact support
of the window handles small D.

128
00:08:37,818 --> 00:08:41,338
Local uniform convergence makes
the transform holomorphic.

129
00:08:43,305 --> 00:08:47,825
In the original region sigma greater
than one, termwise integration

130
00:08:47,865 --> 00:08:53,225
identifies this transform with W hat
of s divided by the Hecke L function,

131
00:08:53,225 --> 00:08:56,265
with finitely many Euler factors deleted.

132
00:08:56,265 --> 00:08:59,945
These factors are nonzero in the
half-plane under discussion.

133
00:08:59,945 --> 00:09:02,585
The identity then extends analytically.

134
00:09:02,585 --> 00:09:06,185
But we still need to prevent the
numerator from accidentally cancelling

135
00:09:06,185 --> 00:09:06,985
a zero.

136
00:09:09,018 --> 00:09:13,498
Suppose rho were a zero to
the right of eleven twelfths.

137
00:09:13,498 --> 00:09:17,898
Choose a nonzero, nonnegative smooth
function phi supported between

138
00:09:17,898 --> 00:09:24,018
one and two, and set W of y equal
to y to minus rho times phi of y.

139
00:09:24,172 --> 00:09:28,698
Then W hat at rho is the
positive integral of phi over y.

140
00:09:28,698 --> 00:09:31,378
The estimate permits this complex window.

141
00:09:31,498 --> 00:09:33,018
Constants may depend on it;

142
00:09:33,018 --> 00:09:34,618
the exponent does not.

143
00:09:36,652 --> 00:09:41,052
Multiplying the continued identity
by L gives L times the holomorphic

144
00:09:41,052 --> 00:09:46,332
transform equals W hat. At the
supposed zero, the left side is zero

145
00:09:46,332 --> 00:09:49,052
while the right side is strictly positive.

146
00:09:49,052 --> 00:09:52,892
This is the contradiction! Notice
what carried the reasoning:

147
00:09:52,892 --> 00:09:57,212
a bound on all scales and analytic
continuation, not checking zeros

148
00:09:57,212 --> 00:10:01,132
numerically and not moving a
contour across an unaccounted pole.

149
00:10:03,603 --> 00:10:07,443
We have derived the boundary
assuming the mean-square theorem.

150
00:10:07,443 --> 00:10:09,603
Now we look inside its proof.

151
00:10:09,603 --> 00:10:13,843
Expanding the square and applying
Poisson summation in the row variable

152
00:10:13,843 --> 00:10:17,603
replaces arithmetic positions
by Fourier frequencies.

153
00:10:17,603 --> 00:10:21,923
Finite Fourier transforms of sextic
characters produce Gauss sums.

154
00:10:21,923 --> 00:10:26,163
A Gauss–Jacobi identity combines
these with the Möbius coefficients,

155
00:10:26,163 --> 00:10:28,643
producing cubic Gauss-sum coefficients.

156
00:10:30,683 --> 00:10:34,843
Those cubic Gauss sums occur as
Fourier coefficients of a cubic theta

157
00:10:34,843 --> 00:10:38,883
function. This supplies transformation
laws that were invisible in

158
00:10:38,883 --> 00:10:43,563
the original sum. To use them, the
paper completes squarefree indices

159
00:10:43,563 --> 00:10:46,643
n to indices n times b cubed.

160
00:10:46,660 --> 00:10:49,323
The extra cube factors are essential.

161
00:10:49,323 --> 00:10:51,483
This diagram is a structural map;

162
00:10:51,483 --> 00:10:55,603
it suppresses local factors,
coprimality conditions, and auxiliary

163
00:10:55,803 --> 00:10:56,403
characters.

164
00:10:58,597 --> 00:11:02,323
After the theta transformation,
the relevant sextic phases combine

165
00:11:02,323 --> 00:11:05,123
into a third power, which is quadratic.

166
00:11:05,123 --> 00:11:09,363
The quadratic large sieve bounds a
mean square using a row-plus-column

167
00:11:09,363 --> 00:11:12,883
scale, M plus L, up to small powers.

168
00:11:12,883 --> 00:11:16,683
This avoids the additional term that
can appear in a general higher-order

169
00:11:16,763 --> 00:11:20,883
character estimate. The gain is
quantitative, not simply a new way

170
00:11:20,883 --> 00:11:22,323
of writing the same sum.

171
00:11:24,303 --> 00:11:28,783
The completed sum includes cube
factors that were not in the target.

172
00:11:28,783 --> 00:11:34,143
Möbius inversion removes them. Small
cube divisors can be bounded directly.

173
00:11:34,143 --> 00:11:38,143
Large cube divisors leave a remainder,
and that remainder still has

174
00:11:38,143 --> 00:11:41,716
to be controlled. This is
where the recursion enters.

175
00:11:41,716 --> 00:11:45,743
The transformation and these remainder
estimates are deep inputs claimed

176
00:11:45,743 --> 00:11:49,903
by the manuscript; our animations
do not independently prove them.

177
00:11:52,368 --> 00:11:56,808
Call the row scale script
H and the column scale X.

178
00:11:56,808 --> 00:12:01,248
In the initial dual problem, they
are about D to one minus theta and

179
00:12:01,248 --> 00:12:06,208
D. A remaining cube divisor of norm
B shortens the column scale to

180
00:12:06,208 --> 00:12:10,768
X over B cubed. Simply shortening
columns is not enough, because the

181
00:12:10,768 --> 00:12:12,688
old row range is still present.

182
00:12:14,634 --> 00:12:19,234
The paper uses two Poisson transformations,
with a deliberate enlargement

183
00:12:19,234 --> 00:12:21,754
of an intermediate nonnegative sum.

184
00:12:21,914 --> 00:12:26,554
The new row scale is script H over
B cubed and the new column scale

185
00:12:26,554 --> 00:12:28,274
is X over B cubed.

186
00:12:28,527 --> 00:12:32,834
Their ratio is unchanged. The
normalization of the energy compensates

187
00:12:32,834 --> 00:12:36,674
for the factor lost during transfer,
so the new target estimate has

188
00:12:36,714 --> 00:12:38,234
exactly the same form.

189
00:12:40,258 --> 00:12:43,641
The cutoff is chosen so that the
remaining branches shrink the row

190
00:12:43,641 --> 00:12:47,641
scale by a factor bounded
by D to minus two theta.

191
00:12:47,641 --> 00:12:51,721
For fixed positive theta, only a
bounded number of stages is needed

192
00:12:51,721 --> 00:12:56,241
before direct counting or an empty
remainder finishes the argument.

193
00:12:56,241 --> 00:12:59,081
This is the simplified
recursion from the outline.

194
00:12:59,081 --> 00:13:03,721
The full proof also carries common
factors, auxiliary twists, and changing

195
00:13:03,721 --> 00:13:04,601
smooth weights.

196
00:13:07,066 --> 00:13:11,346
The September manuscript's stronger
seven-eighths argument is not obtained

197
00:13:11,346 --> 00:13:14,266
by changing the sixth
power in our calculation.

198
00:13:14,266 --> 00:13:18,506
It compares two representations
of a completed cubic-theta sum.

199
00:13:18,506 --> 00:13:21,039
Reflection bounds the sum directly.

200
00:13:21,039 --> 00:13:24,946
Poisson summation separates a
principal signal containing one over

201
00:13:24,946 --> 00:13:28,186
the target L function from
other character rows.

202
00:13:28,186 --> 00:13:32,026
The paper must control both the sum
and the error between the sum and

203
00:13:32,026 --> 00:13:32,826
that signal.

204
00:13:34,880 --> 00:13:39,480
Let beta star be the supremum of
the real parts of zeros across the

205
00:13:39,560 --> 00:13:43,680
relevant Hecke family, with one
half included in the definition.

206
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:47,093
Suppose it exceeds a proposed
boundary sigma zero.

207
00:13:47,093 --> 00:13:50,920
The displayed pair of bounds gives
the signal a positive power saving

208
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,280
below beta star. The minimum of
the two margins must stay positive

209
00:13:55,280 --> 00:13:57,520
independently of the target character.

210
00:13:59,466 --> 00:14:03,306
Taking a Mellin transform of this
better-controlled signal continues

211
00:14:03,306 --> 00:14:07,066
each reciprocal L function into a
common half-plane to the left of

212
00:14:07,066 --> 00:14:12,746
beta star. A holomorphic factor bounded
away from zero prevents cancellation.

213
00:14:12,746 --> 00:14:17,026
By the definition of a supremum,
some zero lies inside that extended

214
00:14:17,226 --> 00:14:20,255
region, even if no zero attains beta star.

215
00:14:20,255 --> 00:14:22,506
Its reciprocal would have a pole there.

216
00:14:22,506 --> 00:14:23,466
Contradiction.

217
00:14:25,433 --> 00:14:29,913
To reach seven eighths, the paper
changes the normalized sum:

218
00:14:29,913 --> 00:14:34,633
it compensates selected prime factors,
uses unequal averaging scales,

219
00:14:34,633 --> 00:14:39,553
and combines moment estimates for inverse
and ordinary character polynomials.

220
00:14:39,553 --> 00:14:44,033
The affine exponent changes from s
minus two thirds to s minus eleven

221
00:14:44,033 --> 00:14:48,393
sixteenths. These estimates close
the argument at seven eighths.

222
00:14:48,393 --> 00:14:52,553
This is a map of the extra machinery,
not a derivation of that exponent

223
00:14:52,553 --> 00:14:54,713
from our eleven-twelfths calculation.

224
00:14:57,244 --> 00:15:01,044
Why did solving the larger number-field
problem answer our original

225
00:15:01,084 --> 00:15:05,804
question? Lift a Dirichlet character
by composing it with the ideal

226
00:15:05,804 --> 00:15:10,404
norm. Its Hecke L function factors
into the Dirichlet L function for

227
00:15:10,484 --> 00:15:15,004
chi and the one for chi times the
quadratic character modulo three,

228
00:15:15,004 --> 00:15:17,764
apart from harmless finite Euler factors.

229
00:15:17,964 --> 00:15:22,084
Nonvanishing of the product
excludes zeros of either factor.

230
00:15:22,084 --> 00:15:24,204
Poles at one are treated separately.

231
00:15:26,164 --> 00:15:31,244
In the explicit formula for weighted
prime counting, a zero rho contributes

232
00:15:31,244 --> 00:15:35,164
a term proportional to x
to rho divided by rho.

233
00:15:35,164 --> 00:15:39,764
Its magnitude contains x to beta,
where beta is the real part.

234
00:15:39,764 --> 00:15:43,764
The graph shows illustrative power
envelopes, not actual prime-counting

235
00:15:43,764 --> 00:15:48,044
errors. Bounding all the betas away
from one creates a fixed power

236
00:15:48,084 --> 00:15:52,564
saving, with further logarithms and
summation estimates handled analytically.

237
00:15:54,531 --> 00:15:59,251
The October paper states this uniform
absolute error for counting primes

238
00:15:59,251 --> 00:16:03,931
in reduced arithmetic progressions:
x to eleven twelfths times log

239
00:16:04,051 --> 00:16:09,251
x. For large moduli, that error can
exceed the main term, so the statement

240
00:16:09,251 --> 00:16:13,651
alone is not a useful relative
asymptotic in every progression.

241
00:16:13,651 --> 00:16:17,811
Both papers also exclude Landau–Siegel
zeros sufficiently close to

242
00:16:17,811 --> 00:16:21,651
one. They do not rule out
every real zero everywhere.

243
00:16:24,130 --> 00:16:26,850
The accessible chain is now concrete.

244
00:16:26,930 --> 00:16:29,610
A mean-square estimate controls a family.

245
00:16:29,730 --> 00:16:32,090
Sixth-power rows repeat the target.

246
00:16:32,330 --> 00:16:34,690
Their count produces eleven twelfths.

247
00:16:34,770 --> 00:16:38,650
A Mellin transform converts
cancellation into holomorphy.

248
00:16:38,650 --> 00:16:42,250
A supposed zero then
forces an impossible pole.

249
00:16:42,250 --> 00:16:46,330
Poisson summation, cubic theta
transformations, and a scale-reducing

250
00:16:46,330 --> 00:16:50,210
recursion are what the paper uses
to earn that initial estimate.

251
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:56,403
The seven-eighths paper uses a more
elaborate comparison and continuation

252
00:16:56,403 --> 00:16:59,763
argument to move the fixed
boundary farther left.

253
00:16:59,763 --> 00:17:03,683
If valid, either fixed boundary
is a historic advance.

254
00:17:03,683 --> 00:17:06,523
Neither statement forces the critical line.

255
00:17:06,540 --> 00:17:10,723
The central lesson is that the power
of the result comes from one guarantee

256
00:17:10,723 --> 00:17:14,043
valid at every height
and for every character.

257
00:17:14,043 --> 00:17:17,683
The accompanying transcript gives
the equations, chapter times, and

258
00:17:17,683 --> 00:17:19,923
exact places to continue reading.
